WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.040
Is "MONSTER" an accurate look at Jeffrey&nbsp;
Dahmer's life and crimes, or does it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.960
sensationalize beyond recognition? Here's the&nbsp;
verdict on Netflix's latest true-crime hit.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.460
The "true" in "true crime" is&nbsp;
sometimes a bit misleading. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:18.360
the stories are based on real events,&nbsp;
but the people behind the stories often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.960
use some dramatic license to make it more&nbsp;
engaging for the viewer, listener, or reader.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:26.400
This was very likely on the mind of Ryan&nbsp;
Murphy, the writer, director, producer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.800
and creator of countless hit TV shows, as he&nbsp;
was developing Netflix's most recent foray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.780
into dramatized true crime, "MONSTER: The&nbsp;
Jeffrey Dahmer Story." Online buzz around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:42.060
the show is already electric, but viewers may be&nbsp;
wondering how much of the story actually happened.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.920
Murphy allegedly has a spotty track record&nbsp;
regarding accuracy in his projects, many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:51.600
which skew toward retelling real events. Whether&nbsp;
chronicling the court cases of O.J. Simpson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:57.240
the impeachment of former president Bill Clinton,&nbsp;
the "feud" between Bette Davis and Joan Crawford,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:01.500
or the murder of Gianni Versace, the&nbsp;
filmmaker is clearly attracted to real-life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:06.900
events that involved lots of drama. While he&nbsp;
definitely sometimes embellishes reality for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:11.460
artistic effect, some have accused him of&nbsp;
stretching the truth beyond recognition.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:16.440
According to Rose Law Group, actor Olivia de&nbsp;
Havilland filed a lawsuit against FX and Murphy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.520
alleging that her portrayal in "Feud"&nbsp;
tarnished her reputation and put words&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:25.140
in her mouth. And the Halston family called&nbsp;
the Ewan McGregor-led drama "Halston," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:29.340
chronicled the titular real-life fashion&nbsp;
designer, "inaccurate" and "fictionalized"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:33.840
in a statement. If Murphy's detractors are&nbsp;
to be believed, it may be challenging to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.760
take his most recent project at face value.&nbsp;
Yet Decider has fact-checked "MONSTER," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.720
their findings draw a unique conclusion&nbsp;
for the true crime genre, proclaiming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:46.800
that it "May Be One of the Most Accurate True&nbsp;
Crime Dramatizations Brought to Television."

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.760
Decider asserts that most of the&nbsp;
series' outrageous and disturbing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:55.620
events actually did occur. This seems&nbsp;
to be especially true for the specter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:00.240
of institutional malpractice haunting this&nbsp;
case. Glenda Cleveland's ignored police call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.460
and the failure to save 14-year-old Konerak&nbsp;
Sinthasomphone are both noted as accurate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:09.120
right down to a gross joke made&nbsp;
by one of the police officers.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.780
"It'll be a minute. My partner's got&nbsp;
to go get deloused at the station."

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:17.160
Decider argues that the show is actually&nbsp;
more compelling by sticking to actual events,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:22.080
even to the point of showcasing an empathetic&nbsp;
side of Dahmer, depicting him as someone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.880
was ultimately failed by the system. Still,&nbsp;
they report that "MONSTER" resists the trope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.860
of glorifying Dahmer, presenting him in&nbsp;
as realistic and unattractive a light as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:36.480
possible. While the show explores the societal&nbsp;
causes and implications of Dahmer's actions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:41.280
the victims whose lives were destroyed by&nbsp;
those very actions are always of paramount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.580
importance. Actor Evan Peters, who&nbsp;
plays Dahmer in the series, said,

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:50.460
"It felt important to be respectful to&nbsp;
the victims, to the victims' families."

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:55.920
Decider does note some small inaccuracies.&nbsp;
The real Dahmer did not drink blood from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.060
bag while working at a plasma center;&nbsp;
he consumed it from a vial. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:03.900
Dahmer's own killer, Christopher Scarver,&nbsp;
is shown to attack fellow inmate Jesse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.860
Anderson before going to Dahmer. In reality,&nbsp;
Scarver stated that he attacked Dahmer first.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:10.680
Historical accuracy feels&nbsp;
especially important when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:14.640
dealing with such a well-known subject&nbsp;
as Jeffrey Dahmer. It's all too easy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.380
forget his victims and conjure a distracting&nbsp;
image of a cinematic criminal mastermind. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:23.100
mythologizing Dahmer would be failing to&nbsp;
learn the lessons of this horrific story.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:27.720
The most recent film about the killer, "My Friend&nbsp;
Dahmer," followed the killer's last years in high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:32.280
school and took a deliberately accurate approach&nbsp;
as well. The graphic novel the film is based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.840
was autobiographical. Its writer, John Backderf,&nbsp;
began writing the story of their friendship after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:41.700
news about Dahmer's crimes became public.&nbsp;
The idiosyncrasies he and others had seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:47.040
and bushed off were suddenly missed signals. In&nbsp;
a 2012 interview with Publishers Weekly, he said,

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:51.300
"It just seems to me that at every crucial&nbsp;
point on his march to the edge of the abyss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:54.180
it was an adult's failure that&nbsp;
propelled him along the way."

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:58.020
These themes of institutional&nbsp;
failure and the consequences of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:01.320
societal neglect are likewise&nbsp;
found in Murphy's "MONSTER."

